1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,630
Your phone is the reason you have no

2
00:00:01,630 --> 00:00:02,450
identity.

3
00:00:02,450 --> 00:00:04,220
I came across this line in a Substack

4
00:00:04,220 --> 00:00:05,730
essay recently, and honestly,

5
00:00:05,730 --> 00:00:06,840
it made me pause.

6
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:08,090
Because think about it,

7
00:00:08,090 --> 00:00:09,870
when was the last time you discovered

8
00:00:09,870 --> 00:00:11,530
something you genuinely liked without

9
00:00:11,530 --> 00:00:12,870
Instagram, YouTube or algorithm

10
00:00:12,870 --> 00:00:14,350
recommending it to you?

11
00:00:14,570 --> 00:00:16,170
Look around. We are watching the same

12
00:00:16,170 --> 00:00:17,870
movies, listening to the same music,

13
00:00:17,870 --> 00:00:19,350
wearing similar clothes,

14
00:00:19,350 --> 00:00:20,930
visiting the same aesthetic cafés,

15
00:00:20,930 --> 00:00:22,430
and sometimes even developing the same

16
00:00:22,430 --> 00:00:23,340
opinions.

17
00:00:23,420 --> 00:00:24,490
And somehow, we still think we are

18
00:00:24,490 --> 00:00:26,340
expressing our individuality.

19
00:00:26,460 --> 00:00:28,370
But here is where it gets interesting.

20
00:00:28,570 --> 00:00:30,120
If you don't spend enough time getting

21
00:00:30,120 --> 00:00:31,330
to know yourself, you will end up

22
00:00:31,330 --> 00:00:33,170
absorbing everyone else's definition

23
00:00:33,170 --> 00:00:33,980
of you.

24
00:00:34,070 --> 00:00:35,170
And I think there is a much bigger

25
00:00:35,170 --> 00:00:36,480
idea behind this.

26
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:37,930
There is a concept in philosophy

27
00:00:37,930 --> 00:00:39,390
called the Culture Industry,

28
00:00:39,390 --> 00:00:42,030
introduced by Theodor Adorno and Max

29
00:00:42,030 --> 00:00:42,980
Horkheimer.

30
00:00:43,180 --> 00:00:44,570
They argued that mass-produced

31
00:00:44,570 --> 00:00:45,730
entertainment could make culture

32
00:00:45,730 --> 00:00:47,300
increasingly standardised.

33
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:49,010
Now think about what algorithms do.

34
00:00:49,190 --> 00:00:50,140
You like something once,

35
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:51,150
and suddenly you are surrounded by

36
00:00:51,150 --> 00:00:52,240
more of the same.

37
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:53,540
Your preferences become predictable,

38
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:54,750
your feed becomes familiar,

39
00:00:54,750 --> 00:00:56,150
and slowly, discovering something

40
00:00:56,150 --> 00:00:57,620
outside the bubble starts feeling

41
00:00:57,620 --> 00:00:58,400
unusual.

42
00:00:58,580 --> 00:01:00,200
But can we really blame algorithms or

43
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:01,360
capitalism? After all,

44
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:02,740
we also choose to consume what they

45
00:01:02,740 --> 00:01:03,580
offer.

46
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:04,900
Maybe the real problem is that we have

47
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:06,360
lost the habit of exploring without

48
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:07,820
being told what to explore.

49
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:08,950
And perhaps the most important

50
00:01:08,950 --> 00:01:10,450
question isn't whether the internet is

51
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:11,850
changing our identity.

52
00:01:11,850 --> 00:01:12,750
It's this:

53
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:14,020
if you stepped away from everyone

54
00:01:14,020 --> 00:01:15,210
else's opinions for a while,

55
00:01:15,210 --> 00:01:16,230
would you still

56
00:01:16,510 --> 00:01:18,220
know what you actually like?
